<.legal_page id="privacy" title="Privacy Policy" flash={@flash} current_user={@current_user}>
  <p>
    This policy explains what Reviews collects when you use the hosted service at <strong>reviews.figitaki.dev</strong>, why, who else receives it, and what you can do about
    it. Reviews is operated by <.operator /> ("we", "us"). The Reviews source code is open source
    under the MIT license; if you run your own copy, you operate it and this policy does not
    apply to it.
  </p>

  <section id="privacy-summary">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You sign in with GitHub. We keep your GitHub user ID, username, avatar URL and email address.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reviews you push, and comments on them, can be read by <strong>anyone who has the link</strong>. Do not push secrets or code you are not allowed to share.
      </li>
      <li>
        We do not sell your data, show ads, or use third-party analytics or tracking scripts.
      </li>
      <li>
        Apps you connect, such as Claude, ChatGPT or Amp, receive the review data they read through Reviews, under their own privacy policies.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-collect">
    <h2>What we collect</h2>

    <h3>Your GitHub account</h3>
    <p>
      Signing in uses GitHub OAuth with the <code>read:user</code>
      and <code>user:email</code>
      scopes. From GitHub we store your numeric GitHub user ID, your username, the URL of your
      GitHub avatar, and your email address. We do not receive your GitHub password and we do not
      ask for access to your repositories. We use your email address only to identify your account
      and to contact you about it; it is not shown on reviews or to other people. It is returned to
      you, and to tools you authorize to act for you, through the API.
    </p>

    <h3>Identities</h3>
    <p>
      Your account has a personal identity, named after your GitHub username, and can own
      <em>agent identities</em>
      you create for AI agents and tools. An agent identity has the handle, display name,
      platform and description you give it. Anything an agent identity does is linked to your
      account.
    </p>

    <h3>Reviews and their content</h3>
    <p>
      When you or an agent push a review, we store what is sent: the diff, the review packet,
      the title and description, the branch name and base commit, and each later revision.
      Diffs can contain source code, file paths, commit details and anything else in the changed
      files.
    </p>

    <h3>Comments and decisions</h3>
    <p>
      We store comments and replies, which identity wrote them and when, where in the code each
      thread is anchored, whether it is resolved and by whom, and the decision each account makes
      on each section of a review. We also store which parts of a review you have marked viewed,
      and your settings, such as display preferences.
    </p>

    <h3>API tokens and CLI sign-ins</h3>
    <p>
      For each API token we store its name, the identity it acts as, when it was created and
      when it was last used. We store only a hash of the token itself, so we cannot show it to you
      again. Signing in the CLI with <code>reviews login</code>
      creates an approval like the connected apps below.
    </p>

    <h3>Connected apps</h3>
    <p>
      You can connect apps such as Claude (Anthropic), ChatGPT (OpenAI), Amp and other OAuth or
      MCP clients so they can read reviews and, if you allow it, act as an agent identity you
      choose. For each approval we store the app's client ID and the name it gives itself, the
      identity and scopes you approved, when you approved it, when it was last used, and when it
      expires or was revoked. We store hashes of the tokens we issue, not the tokens. To verify an
      app, Reviews fetches the metadata and public keys the app publishes at its own web address.
    </p>
    <p>
      Agents that use the Personal Agent Protocol also send signed session information. We store
      the agent's client ID, session and user identifiers it sends, and when the session was last
      seen. Comments such an agent posts record what was verified about it, such as its client ID,
      the name it claims and the key it signed with.
    </p>

    <h3>Code snapshots</h3>
    <p>
      Reviews has an optional feature that uploads the source code at a review's base and head
      commits to our code storage provider, code.storage, so it can be read alongside the diff.
      <strong>It is not turned on for the hosted service.</strong>
      If we turn it on, we will update this policy first.
    </p>

    <h3>Cookies and browser storage</h3>
    <p>
      We set one cookie, <code>_reviews_key</code>, which keeps you signed in and protects forms
      against cross-site requests. It is signed so it cannot be altered, and it lasts until you sign
      out or close your browser. The site also saves a few display choices in your browser's local storage, such as the
      theme and how diffs are shown; these stay on your device. We use no advertising or analytics
      cookies.
    </p>

    <h3>Request logs and rate limiting</h3>
    <p>
      Our servers log each request: the time, method, path, response status, how long it took
      and a request ID. Sign-in codes, tokens and similar secrets are removed from logged
      parameters. To protect the service from abuse, each server counts recent requests per
      network address and per account in memory for about a minute. These counters are keyed by a
      hash and are not written to disk. Our hosting provider also processes network addresses to
      deliver traffic to us, as described in its own privacy policy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-visibility">
    <h2>Who can see your content</h2>
    <p>
      A review is shared by its link: <strong>anyone with the URL can read the review, its diff,
      its packet and its comments, without signing in.</strong>
      Pages of reviews ask search engines not to index them, but this is a request, not access
      control, and anyone who has the link can pass it on. When you paste a review link into an
      app such as Slack or iMessage, that app fetches the page and may show its title and, for a
      comment link, the comment.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your username, avatar and identities' names appear next to what you write. Your email
      address does not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-use">
    <h2>How we use it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        To run Reviews: sign you in, store and show reviews, and record comments and decisions.
      </li>
      <li>To let the CLI, API tokens and connected apps act as the identity you chose.</li>
      <li>
        To keep Reviews secure and available, including rate limiting and investigating abuse.
      </li>
      <li>To understand overall use through the aggregate counts described below.</li>
      <li>To contact you about your account or about changes to these policies.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Our legal bases, where the law requires one, are performing our agreement with you (the
      Terms of Service), our legitimate interest in running and protecting a developer tool, and
      your consent where we ask for it, such as when you connect an app.
      <.placeholder>[CONFIRM: legal bases, if GDPR applies]</.placeholder>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-analytics">
    <h2>Aggregate usage statistics</h2>
    <p>
      Reviews can export a fixed set of totals to a private dashboard we operate: counts of
      accounts, sign-ups, active accounts, reviews, revisions, comments and section decisions over
      the last 7 and 30 days, and how many accounts have connected each recognized app (Claude,
      ChatGPT or Amp, with every other app counted together). The totals are computed inside our database. The export contains
      no review content, comments, names, usernames, email addresses, network addresses or account
      identifiers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-third-parties">
    <h2>Service providers and other parties</h2>
    <p>We do not sell or rent personal information. It reaches these parties:</p>
    <div class="legal-table-wrap">
      <table class="legal-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Party</th>
            <th scope="col">What they receive</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Fly.io</th>
            <td>
              Hosts the application, so it processes everything Reviews stores and receives,
              including network addresses. Our servers run in the United States.
              <.placeholder>[CONFIRM: where the database is hosted]</.placeholder>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">GitHub</th>
            <td>
              Handles sign-in. Avatars are loaded by your browser from GitHub's servers, which
              see your network address. The CLI installer is downloaded from GitHub.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Apps you connect</th>
            <td>
              Claude, ChatGPT, Amp, the Reviews CLI and other OAuth, MCP or Personal Agent
              Protocol clients receive the reviews, comments and account details they request
              with the access you approve.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">code.storage</th>
            <td>Source code snapshots, only if that feature is turned on. It is off today.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Preview deployments</th>
            <td>
              Test copies of Reviews that we run sign in through the main site. If you sign in to
              one, it receives your GitHub user ID, username, avatar URL and email address.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      We may also disclose information if the law requires it, to protect the rights or safety of
      people or the service, or as part of a transfer of the service to a new operator, who would
      remain bound by this policy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-connected-apps">
    <h2>Apps you connect</h2>
    <p>
      When you connect an app, it can read anything on Reviews that its approved scopes cover,
      and write as the agent identity you picked. What the app does with that data, including
      sending it to an AI model, is governed by that app's own terms and privacy policy, not this
      one. You can see and revoke connected apps, CLI sign-ins and API tokens at any time in
      Settings.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-retention">
    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Account data, identities, reviews and comments: until you delete them or ask us to delete your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        Deleted reviews: deleting a review removes its revisions, diffs, packet, comments and decisions. We keep the review's link name so the link can say it was deleted.
      </li>
      <li>
        API tokens: deleted when you revoke them. App and CLI approvals: kept after they are revoked or expire, as a record of what acted for you, until your account is deleted.
      </li>
      <li>Request logs: <.placeholder>[LOG RETENTION PERIOD]</.placeholder>.</li>
      <li>Database backups: <.placeholder>[BACKUP RETENTION PERIOD]</.placeholder>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Reviews has no self-service account deletion yet. To delete your account and everything
      linked to it, email <.contact />.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-security">
    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Connections use HTTPS. Tokens are stored as hashes, and secret values are kept out of
      logs. No system is perfectly secure; if you find a vulnerability, please report it to
      <.contact />.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-rights">
    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can revoke tokens and connected apps in Settings, delete reviews you wrote, and sign out
      at any time. Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct, delete
      or export your personal information, and to object to or restrict how we use it. To make a
      request, email <.contact />. We will answer within the time the law requires. You may also
      complain to your local data protection authority.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-children">
    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      Reviews is a tool for software developers and is not meant for children. You must be at
      least 13, or older where your local law requires, to use it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-transfers">
    <h2>International transfers</h2>
    <p>
      We and our providers operate in the United States. If you use Reviews from elsewhere, your
      information is transferred to and processed in the United States.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-changes">
    <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page when what Reviews collects or shares changes, and change the date
      at the top. For significant changes we will give notice on the site or by email before they
      take effect.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy-contact">
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions or requests: <.contact />, <.operator />.</p>
  </section>
</.legal_page>
